   "text": "The drawing is a progesterone skeleton: a four-ring steroid with a 3-keto-4-ene in ring A, two angular methyl groups and an acetyl group on ring D.\n\n- **Stereochemistry:** the drawing is flipped left to right compared with the usual steroid layout, so the wedges and hashes look reversed. Reading them in that flipped view, they match natural progesterone:\n  - the C10 and C13 methyls are hashed (\u03b2 in the standard view);\n  - the C17 acetyl is hashed (\u03b2);\n  - the C8 H is hashed (\u03b2);\n  - the C9 H and C14 H are wedged (\u03b1).\n- **Rest of the molecule:** a ring A enone (C=C conjugated to the C=O), a methyl ketone (acetyl) at C17, and no other heteroatoms.\n\nI did not re-derive the @/@@ marks atom by atom. I took them from the standard natural-progesterone SMILES, which has the same stereochemical pattern.\n\nSMILES: CC(=O)[C@H]1CC[C@@H]2[C@@]1(CC[C@H]3[C@H]2CCC4=CC(=O)CC[C@]34C)C"
